# Appendix D — The Tick, the Scheduler & Game State

Chapter 5 gave you the tick and Chapter 13 built a game around it. This appendix
is the model underneath: what one `world/step` actually does, why a game is really
a *state machine*, and how you write those states in plain Motoi Scheme — because
there is no state-machine *library*, and there does not need to be.

## One `world/step` moves time forward one frame

The world holds things with positions and velocities. A `world/step` is one frame:
every thing with a velocity moves by that velocity, once. Give a hero a rightward
velocity and step twice:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define h (world/spawn 'hero 0 10))
(entity/set-vel! h 5 0)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display (entity/x h))
(newline)
```

```
10
```

The hero started at `x = 0` with velocity `5`. Two steps, two moves of `5`, and
`entity/x` reads `10`. That is the entire scheduler in one fact: **`world/step` is
the crank, and turning it advances every moving thing by one tick's worth.** In a
live console the Game door turns this crank sixty times a second via `on-tick`. At
the bare prompt you turn it yourself, which is why every game in this book runs
the same whether the console pumps the loop or you do.

## The census: what is in the world right now

The scheduler also keeps count. `world/count` asks the world how many of a kind of
thing it currently holds — the same census Chapter 13 used to detect a win:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/spawn 'coin 10 10)
(world/spawn 'coin 30 10)
(display (world/count 'coin))
(newline)
```

```
2
```

Two coins spawned, `world/count` reads `2`. Despawn one and it reads `1`; despawn
both and it reads `0`. The world counts itself, so your win condition never has to
keep a separate tally that could drift out of sync — you ask the scheduler, and it
tells you the truth about the current frame.

## A game is a state machine

Here is the idea that ties every game together. At any instant your game is in one
*state* — a title screen, playing, paused, game-over — and each tick may move it to
a new state based on what happened. That is a **state machine**: a set of named
states and the rules for moving between them.

Motoi has **no state-machine library** — no `fsm/make`, no `state/` namespace. It
does not need one, because plain Scheme already is one. A state is a variable; a
transition is a `cond`; the tick is what runs the `cond`:

```motoi
(define state 'idle)
(define (tick)
  (set! state
    (cond ((eq? state 'idle) 'run)
          ((eq? state 'run)  'done)
          (else state))))
(tick)
(tick)
(display state)
(newline)
```

```
done
```

The game began `idle`. The first `tick` read the rule for `idle` and moved to
`run`; the second moved `run` to `done`; `done` has no exit, so it stays. That is a
three-state machine — `idle → run → done` — written with nothing but `define`,
`set!`, and `cond`. Add states by adding clauses; add transitions by adding tests.
A pause screen is one more state; a boss fight is one more state. The whole
architecture of a big game is this pattern, wider.

## Why this is the honest way to teach it

A state-machine DSL would hide the `cond` behind a verb and make it look like
magic. Motoi keeps it in the open: you can *see* every state and every transition
because you wrote them as ordinary code. When a game misbehaves, you read the
`cond` and find the wrong clause. There is nothing under the pattern but the
language you already know — which is exactly why you can debug it.

> **Tip**
>
> Keep your state in ONE variable and change it in ONE place — a single `tick`
> that runs the `cond`. When every transition lives in one function, the whole
> life of your game is readable top to bottom. Scatter `set!` calls across ten
> functions and you have built a machine nobody, including you, can follow.

> **Warning**
>
> `world/step` moves things by their velocity; it does not read the player's
> buttons or redraw the screen for you — those are separate calls a live console
> makes around each step. At the bare prompt, stepping the world advances physics
> and nothing else. If you expect input or drawing to happen inside `world/step`,
> that is the misread; the step is only the physics tick.

